[217.77189, "i", "cat > /home/travis/build/failed/failed_reasons.txt << 'EOF'\nBuild Failure Analysis for google/auto\n========================================\n\nError: Could not resolve dependencies for project com.google.auto:auto-common:jar:1.0-SNAPSHOT\nSpecific Error: Could not find artifact com.sun:tools:jar:11.0.27 at specified path /usr/lib/jvm/java-11-openjdk-amd64/../lib/tools.jar\n\nRoot Cause:\n-----------\n1. The project is being built with Java 11 (OpenJDK 11.0.27)\n2. The compile-testing:0.7 dependency (used in common/pom.xml as a test dependency) has a system-scoped dependency on com.sun:tools\n3. This dependency uses ${toolsjar} as the systemPath\n4. The toolsjar property is set in profiles that activate based on file existence:\n   - default-profile: checks for ${java.home}/../lib/tools.jar\n   - mac-profile: checks for ${java.home}/../Classes/classes.jar\n5. In Java 9+, tools.jar was removed and its contents were moved into the JDK modules\n6. Neither profile activates in Java 11, so ${toolsjar} is undefined\n7. Maven tries"]
[217.771968, "i", " to resolve the dependency but fails because the path is invalid\n\nSolution:\n---------\nUpdate compile-testing to version 0.10 or later which supports Java 9+ and doesn't require tools.jar.\nAlternatively, we could add a Java 9+ profile that sets toolsjar to a dummy value or excludes the dependency.\n\nThe simplest fix is to update common/pom.xml to use compile-testing version 0.10 instead of 0.7.\nEOF\n"]
[217.772079, "o", "cat > /home/travis/build/failed/failed_reasons.txt << 'EOF'\r\n"]
[217.772159, "o", "> "]
[217.772236, "o", "Build Failure Analysis for google/auto\r\n"]
[217.772268, "o", "> "]
[217.772352, "o", "========================================\r\n"]
[217.772381, "o", "> "]
